Derek Ray wrote:
> On 2007-12-10, Martin Read <mpread@chiark.greenend.org.uk> wrote:
>>
>>3rd edition made it much easier to get *a* bonus (since you only need a
>>12 in a stat to get a bonus from it, where most stats in 1st and 2nd
>>edition gave little direct benefit until about 15-16), and had
>>relatively little effect on the benefits of a score of 18.
> 
> I think this might be a good spot to raise the question: How much do
> stats really matter in Nethack, after a certain point?  (Assume we're
> discussing vanilla here.)

"after a certain point" misses the point. Look at the spoilers to see
what I mean; http://www.spod-central.org/~psmith/nh/stat-343.txt

Given the variety of _initial_ stats per role (and max stat per race) I
think this question doesn't hit the point. The variety is already there
and influences a lot the character classes/races. Until the mid game
the characters try to enhance their stat weaknesses, e.g. to carry more
junk around, to get better spellbook reading capabilities, etc.

> I mean, STR/CON are helpful to increase carrying capacity, [...]
> 
> INT/WIS are for the casters, [...]
> 
> DEX is nice for hitting things, [...]
> 
> And that leaves CHA, of course, the "nearly meaningless stat".

Cha helps certain classes in the early game, and in some encounters in
the mid game.

Generally it doesn't change the point if there's one or the other item
in the game that can compensate any low stat (*if* you have those items).

> While it's all well and good to worry that it may be too easy to go from
> 16 to 17 by simply pushing rocks, the real question might be: does it
> even matter that someone can go from 16 to 17?

Not that much, I suppose. But it matters to increase low initial stats.

Capping stats at 15? That would mean leveling the variety starting from
the early game. No, that's really a bad idea. (And quite arbitrary.)

